1
00:00:01,140 --> 00:00:05,060
Four thousand two hundred years ago, a people
from east of Mesopotamia stepped onto the

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:06,280
stage of history:

3
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:07,420
the Gutians.

4
00:00:07,460 --> 00:00:11,700
They brought Akkad, the world's first
empire, to its knees and ruled Mesopotamia,

5
00:00:11,740 --> 00:00:14,360
the cradle of civilization,
for a full century.

6
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:17,320
Sumerian scribes gave
them a name in fear: the

7
00:00:17,420 --> 00:00:18,980
Dragon of the Mountains.

8
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:20,840
So who were these Gutians?

9
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:24,840
How did they rule Mesopotamia, and
why do so many historians count

10
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:27,500
them among the oldest
ancestors of the Kurds?

11
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:29,080
Welcome to Dîrok.

12
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:44,920
The land between the Tigris and the
Euphrates witnessed the greatest

13
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:46,540
inventions in human history.

14
00:00:46,580 --> 00:00:50,820
The first cities, writing, the wheel
and written law were all born here.

15
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:55,620
Sumerian cities such as Uruk, Ur,
Lagash and Nippur were the world's first

16
00:00:55,660 --> 00:00:56,720
centers of civilization.

17
00:00:56,800 --> 00:01:01,020
In the twenty-third century BC, Sargon
of Akkad conquered these cities one

18
00:01:01,060 --> 00:01:04,580
by one and founded the first
known empire in history.

19
00:01:04,620 --> 00:01:08,620
The Akkadian Empire stretched from
the Persian Gulf to the Mediterranean.

20
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:12,660
Sargon's grandson Naram-Sin called
himself king of the four corners of the

21
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:13,700
world.

22
00:01:13,980 --> 00:01:17,800
But the end of this great
empire would come from the east.

23
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:27,560
Mesopotamian sources call the land
east of the Tigris, between the Lesser

24
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:30,020
Zab and the Diyala rivers, Gutium.

25
00:01:30,060 --> 00:01:34,100
This was the homeland of the
Gutians, and the roads descending
to the Mesopotamian plain were

26
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:35,160
under their control.

27
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:39,360
Under Naram-Sin's son Shar-kali-sharri,
the struggle between Akkad and the

28
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:40,660
Gutians intensified.

29
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:44,180
On Akkadian tablets, years were named
after the important events that took

30
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:45,220
place in them.

31
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:47,660
One of these year names reads:

32
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:51,600
"The year Shar-kali-sharri
captured Sharlag, king of Gutium."

33
00:01:51,740 --> 00:01:54,080
But Akkad's victories did not last.

34
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:58,160
The empire was shaken by revolts,
struggles for the throne and

35
00:01:58,200 --> 00:01:59,500
a great drought.

36
00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:03,760
After Shar-kali-sharri's death, Akkad
fell into such chaos that the Sumerian

37
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:07,100
King List describes the period
with a mocking question.

38
00:02:07,140 --> 00:02:09,340
"Who was king, who was not king?"

39
00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:13,460
The famous Sumerian text "The Curse of
Agade" blames Akkad's fall on the wrath

40
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:17,940
of the gods, and the people sent
to carry out that wrath are named.

41
00:02:17,980 --> 00:02:18,660
The Gutians.

42
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:22,100
The text describes them as a
people who could not be controlled.

43
00:02:22,140 --> 00:02:26,660
Around 2150 BC the Akkadian Empire
collapsed, and the royal cities

44
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:30,120
of Mesopotamia passed into
the hands of the Gutians.

45
00:02:35,540 --> 00:02:38,440
The Sumerian King List
contains a plain record.

46
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:40,800
"Kingship passed to the army of Gutium."

47
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:44,640
The list describes the first period of
the Gutians with a curious sentence.

48
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:47,980
"In the army of Gutium
there was at first no

49
00:02:48,020 --> 00:02:49,860
known king; they were their own kings."

50
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:54,260
Many scholars read this sentence as
a sign that the Gutians were governed

51
00:02:54,300 --> 00:02:58,100
not by a single absolute ruler
but by a shared council or

52
00:02:58,140 --> 00:02:59,140
by tribal leaders.

53
00:02:59,180 --> 00:03:03,500
According to the list, twenty-one Gutian
kings reigned for about a hundred years.

54
00:03:03,540 --> 00:03:06,480
Most of their kings stayed on
the throne for only a few years.

55
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:09,040
This suggests that
leadership did not pass from

56
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:11,860
father to son but changed
hands among the tribes.

57
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:15,720
The Gutians governed the Sumerian
cities through local governors.

58
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:20,300
Lugal-annatum, governor of the city of
Umma, dated his own inscription "in the

59
00:03:20,340 --> 00:03:22,220
time of Si'um, king of Gutium."

60
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:26,400
In other words, the cities kept
their own administrations but
recognized the sovereignty of the

61
00:03:26,440 --> 00:03:26,980
Gutian king.

62
00:03:27,020 --> 00:03:30,480
The Gutians also took over Akkad's
language of government and its traditions.

63
00:03:30,520 --> 00:03:35,040
A mace head belonging to King La'erabum
reads, in Akkadian, "the mighty king

64
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:36,220
of Gutium."

65
00:03:36,260 --> 00:03:39,340
The most remarkable of
these kings is Erridupizir.

66
00:03:39,380 --> 00:03:43,400
In his inscriptions in Nippur,
the holiest city of Mesopotamia,
he introduces himself like this:

67
00:03:43,440 --> 00:03:44,100
"King

68
00:03:44,140 --> 00:03:47,360
of Gutium, king of the
four corners of the world."

69
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:51,020
This is the very title used
by the Akkadian emperors.

70
00:03:51,060 --> 00:03:54,660
His inscriptions describe his campaigns
against rebellious regions and how

71
00:03:54,700 --> 00:03:58,440
he dedicated the spoils to
the temple of the god Enlil.

72
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:01,780
Under the Gutians, the Sumerian
cities also enjoyed a brilliant era.

73
00:04:01,820 --> 00:04:06,200
Lagash, under Prince Gudea, built
temples and lived through a golden age

74
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:07,120
of art.

75
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:10,460
So the Gutians were not raiders
who plundered and withdrew.

76
00:04:10,500 --> 00:04:14,180
They were rulers who inherited the
imperial legacy of Mesopotamia.

77
00:04:14,220 --> 00:04:18,620
Leaders chosen by council, a confederation
of tribes and local autonomy...

78
00:04:18,820 --> 00:04:22,920
This is exactly the picture we will
meet thousands of years later in the

79
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:24,800
social structure of the Kurds.

80
00:04:30,220 --> 00:04:33,100
About a hundred years
later, Utu-hengal, king of

81
00:04:33,140 --> 00:04:35,640
Uruk, launched a great
revolt against the Gutians.

82
00:04:35,680 --> 00:04:38,060
In his victory inscription
he speaks of the Gutians like

83
00:04:38,100 --> 00:04:41,480
this: "Gutium, the fanged
serpent of the mountains,

84
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:43,160
the dragon of the mountains."

85
00:04:43,380 --> 00:04:46,360
At that time the last Gutian
king, Tirigan, was on the throne.

86
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:50,460
According to the Sumerian King List,
Tirigan reigned for only forty days.

87
00:04:50,580 --> 00:04:52,860
Utu-hengal marched north with his army.

88
00:04:52,900 --> 00:04:56,180
He defeated and captured the two
commanders Tirigan sent against him.

89
00:04:56,220 --> 00:05:00,340
At the end of the war, Tirigan abandoned
his army and fled to a city called Dubrum.

90
00:05:00,380 --> 00:05:04,220
But the people of Dubrum seized him
and handed him over to Utu-hengal.

91
00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:08,940
Soon after, Ur-Nammu founded a new dynasty
in the city of Ur, but the struggle did

92
00:05:08,980 --> 00:05:09,600
not end.

93
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:13,580
According to some later Sumerian
texts, even Ur-Nammu lost his life

94
00:05:13,620 --> 00:05:15,820
in a battle against the Gutians.

95
00:05:15,860 --> 00:05:19,440
The political rule of the Gutians
in Mesopotamia had come to an end.

96
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:21,040
But the Gutians did not disappear.

97
00:05:26,460 --> 00:05:30,580
For the next fifteen hundred years,
the name Gutium never vanished

98
00:05:30,620 --> 00:05:31,740
from Mesopotamian sources.

99
00:05:31,780 --> 00:05:35,479
Hammurabi, king of Babylon, recorded
his victories over the army of

100
00:05:35,519 --> 00:05:36,280
Gutium.

101
00:05:36,320 --> 00:05:39,460
Assyrian kings led campaigns
into the land of Qutu.

102
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:43,980
When the Persian king Cyrus
the Great took Babylon in 539

103
00:05:44,020 --> 00:05:47,760
BC, he listed Gutium among the
lands under his rule on the

104
00:05:47,800 --> 00:05:48,480
Cyrus Cylinder.

105
00:05:48,520 --> 00:05:52,200
And the first commander to enter
Babylon was Ugbaru, governor of Gutium.

106
00:05:52,240 --> 00:05:56,240
So for seventeen hundred years
the name Gutium went on describing

107
00:05:56,280 --> 00:05:58,140
the people east of Mesopotamia.

108
00:06:03,560 --> 00:06:06,740
The Gutians were not the only
ancient people living in these lands.

109
00:06:06,780 --> 00:06:11,260
The Lullubi, the Hurrians, the Kassites
and the Medes also lived to the north

110
00:06:11,300 --> 00:06:12,500
and east of Mesopotamia.

111
00:06:12,540 --> 00:06:16,540
According to many historians,
these peoples, together with
the Gutians, are the layers that

112
00:06:16,580 --> 00:06:17,540
formed the Kurds.

113
00:06:17,580 --> 00:06:21,340
Foremost among those who voiced
this link most strongly are foreign

114
00:06:21,380 --> 00:06:21,880
historians.

115
00:06:21,920 --> 00:06:25,060
The orientalist Arshak Safrastian,
working in England, sees the Gutians

116
00:06:25,100 --> 00:06:29,940
directly as the ancestors of the Kurds
in his book published in 1948, and

117
00:06:29,980 --> 00:06:33,800
links the name Guti to
the root of the name Kurd.

118
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:36,280
The chain of names is truly striking.

119
00:06:36,320 --> 00:06:40,560
Assyrian records mention names such as
Qurti and Kurti for the peoples of this

120
00:06:40,600 --> 00:06:41,140
region.

121
00:06:41,180 --> 00:06:45,000
The Greek commander Xenophon writes
of the warlike Kardouchoi living

122
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:46,480
north of the Tigris.

123
00:06:46,520 --> 00:06:49,840
In Roman times the same
region was known as Corduene.

124
00:06:50,420 --> 00:06:52,260
G. R. Driver of Oxford

125
00:06:52,300 --> 00:06:56,040
University studied how the name
Kurd relates to these ancient names.

126
00:06:56,120 --> 00:07:00,320
Vladimir Minorsky emphasized the role of
the Medes in the formation of the Kurds.

127
00:07:00,420 --> 00:07:04,640
Ephraim Speiser described the Gutians
as the native people of the lands east

128
00:07:04,680 --> 00:07:05,520
of Mesopotamia.

129
00:07:05,560 --> 00:07:09,540
Mehrdad Izady argues that the Gutians
and the other ancient peoples form

130
00:07:09,580 --> 00:07:11,300
the foundation of the Kurdish people.

131
00:07:11,340 --> 00:07:14,720
The Kurdish historian Muhammad
Amin Zaki Beg holds the same view.

132
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:17,140
Of course, there are
opposing views as well.

133
00:07:17,180 --> 00:07:21,380
The main reason is that very few documents
in the Gutian language have survived.

134
00:07:21,420 --> 00:07:23,980
All we have are a few names of kings.

135
00:07:24,120 --> 00:07:28,820
Yet geography, social structure and the
continuity of names over thousands of years

136
00:07:28,860 --> 00:07:30,300
make this thesis strong.

137
00:07:31,520 --> 00:07:34,960
The plains of Mesopotamia
were rich but defenseless.

138
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,800
Empires came, cities were burned.

139
00:07:37,860 --> 00:07:42,000
In the thirteenth century the
Mongols razed Baghdad and all of

140
00:07:42,040 --> 00:07:42,540
Mesopotamia.

141
00:07:42,580 --> 00:07:46,720
But thanks to the strategic
advantage of the mountains, the Kurds
were not hit by this destruction

142
00:07:46,760 --> 00:07:48,820
as hard as the peoples of the plains.

143
00:07:48,900 --> 00:07:50,860
The Kurds have an ancient saying:

144
00:07:51,160 --> 00:07:54,804
Ji çiyan pê ve, hevalên me tune.

145
00:07:55,004 --> 00:07:56,964
We have no friends but the mountains.

146
00:07:57,203 --> 00:08:01,404
The Gutians were the people who brought
down Mesopotamia's first empire and

147
00:08:01,444 --> 00:08:02,524
inherited its legacy.

148
00:08:02,564 --> 00:08:06,684
One of the first pages of Kurdish history
was written right here, in these lands.

149
00:08:06,984 --> 00:08:10,764
If you enjoyed this video, don't
forget to subscribe and share your

150
00:08:10,804 --> 00:08:12,184
thoughts in the comments.
